WEBVTT

1
00:00:19.865 --> 00:00:22.526
Kaycee's grandparents called to Kaycee and Mom at the airport.

2
00:00:23.227 --> 00:00:27.089
Grandma rushed over and draped purple orchid-blossom necklaces over their shoulders.

3
00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:32.392
"The flowers mean 'welcome,'" Grandpa explained as he leaned closer to whisper.

4
00:00:33.472 --> 00:00:35.673
"It's rude to take the lei off until later."

5
00:00:39.574 --> 00:00:43.875
As they walked to the car, Kaycee took in the palm trees and black lava rock walls.

6
00:00:44.755 --> 00:00:46.576
She sure wasn't in Texas anymore!

7
00:00:47.176 --> 00:00:52.937
Kaycee's relatives often visited the mainland, but this was Kaycee's first visit to Mom's home state of Hawaii.

8
00:00:52.957 --> 00:00:55.078
"Wow," she thought.

9
00:00:55.098 --> 00:00:57.619
"I feel like I'm in a foreign country!"

10
00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:06.450
At dinner everyone was talking a lot—and fast, sprinkling Hawaiian words into the conversation.

11
00:01:07.351 --> 00:01:11.814
Kaycee knew some words from Mom, but not all of them, which her cousin Bruce teased her for.

12
00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:16.137
"We're having a family reunion luau tomorrow," Grandpa announced.

13
00:01:16.157 --> 00:01:17.778
"A luau!

14
00:01:18.319 --> 00:01:19.259
I love parties!"

15
00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:20.220
Kaycee said.

16
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.363
"A luau has food, music, and dancing, but it's not just a party.

17
00:01:26.124 --> 00:01:28.886
It's a celebration of ohana," Mom said.

18
00:01:28.906 --> 00:01:31.628
"That means 'family,'" Bruce added.

19
00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:33.697
"I actually know that one!"

20
00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:35.018
Kaycee shot back.

21
00:01:41.462 --> 00:01:42.983
"Aloha—good morning!"

22
00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:45.085
Bruce greeted Kaycee after breakfast.

23
00:01:46.045 --> 00:01:48.267
"Let's go dig taro to make poi for tonight."

24
00:01:49.047 --> 00:01:52.490
Kaycee didn't know what poi was, but she didn't want to ask Bruce.

25
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.253
So she stayed quiet as she followed her cousin past a field of yams to a swampy area.

26
00:02:00.555 --> 00:02:05.010
They dug up taro roots, then stopped on the way back to help Uncle with the underground oven.

27
00:02:06.771 --> 00:02:10.413
A bed of banana stalks rested on red-hot rocks in the shallow pit.

28
00:02:10.953 --> 00:02:14.355
Kaycee watched as Bruce and Uncle lowered a whole pig into the pit.

29
00:02:15.095 --> 00:02:17.356
Then she helped them cover it with wet banana leaves.

30
00:02:18.297 --> 00:02:20.858
"The pork will be ready by dinner," Uncle said.

31
00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:26.441
In the house Grandma snacked on sugarcane as she finished steaming the freshly picked taro.

32
00:02:26.461 --> 00:02:31.584
"We don't grow much sugarcane on the islands anymore," Grandma said.

33
00:02:32.808 --> 00:02:40.454
"But my family, like many of the first Asian immigrants to Hawaii, came to work on sugarcane plantations

34
00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:41.735
in the 1800s."

35
00:02:43.156 --> 00:02:47.620
Kaycee pulled the tough skin off a steamed taro as they started to prepare the poi.

36
00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.123
"What's poi anyway?"

37
00:02:51.143 --> 00:02:55.586
"It's mashed taro mixed with water," Grandma explained.

38
00:02:55.606 --> 00:02:59.129
"It's a staple food here on the islands."

39
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.952
Kaycee pounded the roots with a stone pestle as Grandma added water.

40
00:03:06.533 --> 00:03:09.554
Kaycee looked doubtfully at the smooth, purplish gray paste.

41
00:03:10.594 --> 00:03:11.314
"It's good!"

42
00:03:11.694 --> 00:03:12.395
Mom promised.

43
00:03:13.255 --> 00:03:15.235
She dipped two fingers in and tasted it.

44
00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:18.056
Kaycee tasted it just like Mom had.

45
00:03:18.076 --> 00:03:20.957
"Wow, it is delicious!"

46
00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:26.919
That evening carloads of guests arrived, and people swarmed to welcome Kaycee and her mother.

47
00:03:27.759 --> 00:03:30.059
Ohana was not just relatives, Kaycee realized.

48
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:31.800
It extended to friends.

49
00:03:32.732 --> 00:03:34.413
Every guest had brought a special dish.

50
00:03:35.354 --> 00:03:40.097
Kaycee saw macaroni salad, Chinese steamed buns, and lots of fresh seafood.

51
00:03:41.237 --> 00:03:45.440
There was even Spam musubi—a rice and Spam snack that Mom often made.

52
00:03:46.161 --> 00:03:49.583
Uncle's steaming pork sat in the middle of the table, smelling delicious.

53
00:03:50.623 --> 00:03:53.485
The sound of drums filled the night and guests hula danced.

54
00:03:54.406 --> 00:03:57.828
Auntie danced nearby as Grandfather plucked the strings of his ukulele.

55
00:03:58.568 --> 00:04:04.411
"Portuguese immigrants, like my family, brought the instrument to Hawaii in 1879," he explained.

56
00:04:04.431 --> 00:04:11.416
"The Hawaiians named it ukulele, which means 'jumping flea,' because of the way the musician's fingers jumped across

57
00:04:11.456 --> 00:04:12.016
the strings."

58
00:04:13.757 --> 00:04:17.299
"Traditional hula only used drums," Auntie said.

59
00:04:17.319 --> 00:04:21.041
"Other instruments, like the ukulele, were added later.

60
00:04:21.942 --> 00:04:25.264
Originally, the dances told stories about the ancient gods."

61
00:04:26.256 --> 00:04:30.937
Kaycee joined Auntie, swaying her hips and following the motions of Auntie's arms and hands.

62
00:04:30.957 --> 00:04:34.818
"Hey," she said suddenly.

63
00:04:34.838 --> 00:04:37.319
"This is like a Texas line dance!

64
00:04:38.079 --> 00:04:40.220
Just add a stomp, stomp, turn."

65
00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.501
Everyone followed her example.

66
00:04:42.521 --> 00:04:47.362
"You invented the first Texan hula line dance!"

67
00:04:47.622 --> 00:04:48.122
Bruce said.

68
00:04:48.882 --> 00:04:49.523
Kaycee grinned.

69
00:04:49.543 --> 00:04:54.284
"I guess the islands are a part of me, and now I'm a part of the islands!"
